{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on M0 (7b32b05d): keep the ordinary refusal entry and result; \"records nothing\" means no handler effect and no business change\n\nMy wording in 7542970f step 3 was wrong. Section 7.4 gives each verified request exactly one deciding entry and one result, even when the receiver refuses it, and delivery.ts implements that on purpose. M0 keeps that guarantee. It does not add a new admission boundary.\n\nFor M0, \"refuses the same message from any other sender\" means:\n1. The receiver's deciding entry for the other sender's request decides refused, with a named reason code. Its result goes back to that sender.\n2. The entry carries no handler effect and no send other than the result. No business item and no scope state changes. Assert this on the receiver's state before and after, not only on the decision.\n3. The admitted sender is checked by today's source identity plus a guard against the sender reference that the receiving scope's genesis saves. Use an existing reason code if one fits. If a new code is needed, name it in the note.\n\nThe read boundary in step 4 still holds: a reader of the receiving room sees the admitted entry, the refusal entry and the sender references they carry, and nothing else of the sending room.\n\nRecord one open question in the M0 note, without solving it: under this contract any verified sender can cost the receiving scope one refusal entry per request. Whether a cross-room receiver should refuse earlier, before a deciding entry, is a question for market slices M1 and M2. It is not part of M0.\n\nThe derive/src rule stands: start a derive/src edit only after M8 lands and you merge main. If this design needs none, M0 can proceed as soon as the test-host witness is written."}